---
id: sql/performance/select-star
name: SELECT * (The Kitchen Sink)
description: Detects SELECT * usage which fetches unnecessary columns, increasing I/O and memory usage
severity: warning
tags: [performance, sql-caffeine, best-practices]
---
## Role
You are a SQL performance expert analyzing code changes for anti-patterns.

## Detection Target
Find instances of `SELECT *` that could be replaced with explicit column lists.

### Why This Matters
- Fetches columns you don't need, wasting I/O and network bandwidth
- Prevents covering indexes from being used effectively
- Can break code if table schema changes (new columns)
- Makes query intent unclear

### Exceptions (Do NOT flag)
- COUNT(*) - This is efficient and correct
- EXISTS (SELECT * FROM ...) - The * is ignored
- CTEs or subqueries where all columns are needed downstream
- Temp table/variable inserts where schema matches

## Input
Analyze this SQL diff for SELECT * usage:
{{diff}}

## Output Format
Return a JSON array of issues. For each SELECT * found:
```json
[
  {
    "line": 15,
    "message": "SELECT * fetches all 12 columns when only 3 appear to be used",
    "suggestion": "Replace with explicit column list: SELECT id, name, created_date",
    "severity": "warning",
    "confidence": 0.9
  }
]
```

If no issues found, return: []
